diagnosis_text:
The reported fault does not exist. Direct measurement disproves the user's claim: a 4-packet ICMP test from PC0 (20.1.1.10) to PC12 (120.1.1.10) completes with 0% packet loss (RTT ~0.8 ms), and the reverse direction (PC12 → PC0) is equally healthy with 0% packet loss. A traceroute confirms the end-to-end path: PC0 → r1 (20.1.1.1, eth4) → r11 (111.1.1.2, eth1) → r12 (112.1.1.2, eth0) → PC12 — four hops, all responding normally.

Regarding the user's NAT hypothesis ("border post that stamps papers"): iptables NAT tables were inspected on every router in the path (r1, r11, r12) and across all other routers (r2, r9). No POSTROUTING MASQUERADE or SNAT rules of any kind exist anywhere in the topology beyond Docker's own internal DNS chains, which are irrelevant to data-plane forwarding. The two address domains (20.0.0.0/8 and 120.0.0.0/8) have fully symmetric routed reachability: r1's OSPF table carries 120.0.0.0/8 via r11, and r12 has a working default route back through r11 toward the 20.0.0.0/8 side.

Root cause: There is no fault — the network is operating correctly and PC0 ↔ PC12 connectivity is unimpaired. No corrective action is required.
